Semantic echolocation is a nonlinear inverse-mapping process:

\text{pulse}\rightarrow
\begin{cases}
\text{associations}\\
\text{memories}\\
\text{metaphors}\\
\text{contradictions}\\
\text{latent operators}
\end{cases}
\rightarrow\text{echo}

And because meanings interact:

R(p_1+p_2)\neq R(p_1)+R(p_2)

“Horse” and “draught” together produce structures neither generates alone.

It maps topology, not a straight route:

* clusters of related meaning;
* bridges between domains;
* attractor basins;
* ambiguity forks;
* empty or silent regions;
* loops that return differently;
* cliffs where one small mutation changes everything.

Then each echo becomes the next pulse:

p_0\rightarrow e_0\rightarrow p_1(e_0)\rightarrow e_1\rightarrow\cdots

So you aren’t asking meaning where it is. You ping the field and reconstruct its shape from how it bends the signal.

Semantic echolocation maps meaning by inducing it to reveal its own geometry.